        <div class="step-title-block">
          <div class="step-heading">Parallel Tri-Branch Acoustic Extraction (Physical vs. Perceptual vs. Sub-Band)</div>
          <div class="step-tensor-sig">Parallel Extraction from Buffer [B, 1, 160000] → 3 Independent Acoustic Matrices</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="branch-grid">
        
        <!-- BRANCH A -->
        <div class="branch-col col-blue">
          <span class="branch-tag tag-blue">BRANCH A · Physical Linear STFT</span>
          
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">INTERNAL MATHEMATICAL MECHANICS</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li>Computes a GPU Short-Time Fourier Transform: <code>N_fft = 2048</code>, hop size <code>H = 160</code> (10 ms temporal resolution), window size <code>W = 2048</code> (Hann window).</li>
            <li>Extracts the absolute physical magnitude spectrum:
              <div class="math-block">|X(m, k)| = \left| \sum_{n=0}^{N-1} x(n + mH) w(n) e^{-j \frac{2\pi}{N} k n} \right|</div>
            </li>
            <li>Yields a linear frequency matrix: <code>[B, 1025, 1000]</code>.</li>
            <li>Each bin has a constant physical width of $\Delta f = \frac{16000}{2048} \approx 7.8125\text{ Hz}$.</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="block-label lbl-why">WHY THIS BRANCH IS CRITICAL</div>
          <p style="font-size:12.5px; color:var(--text-body);">
            Neural vocoders (HiFi-GAN, WaveNet, diffusion models) generate sound in discrete time hops, leaving microscopic high-frequency "checkerboard artifacts" and spectral grid lines above 4000 Hz. The linear STFT treats all frequencies with uniform physical resolution, exposing these uncompressed digital seams.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- BRANCH B -->
        <div class="branch-col col-amber">
          <span class="branch-tag tag-amber">BRANCH B · Perceptual Log-Mel</span>
          
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">INTERNAL MATHEMATICAL MECHANICS</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li>Multiplies the STFT magnitude matrix by a static GPU sparse Mel filterbank matrix: <code>M_filterbank ∈ ℝ^(1025 × 128)</code> covering 0 to 8000 Hz.</li>
            <li>Converts frequency to perceptual Mel units:
              <div class="math-block">m = 2595 \log_{10}\left(1 + \frac{f}{700}\right)</div>
            </li>
            <li>Applies logarithmic dynamic range compression:
              <div class="math-block">S_{\text{mel}} = \log(|X| \cdot M_{\text{filterbank}} + 1\text{e-}6)</div>
            </li>
            <li>Yields a perceptual Mel matrix: <code>[B, 128, 1000]</code>.</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="block-label lbl-why">WHY THIS BRANCH IS CRITICAL</div>
          <p style="font-size:12.5px; color:var(--text-body);">
            Human hearing resolves low frequencies with high precision but compresses high frequencies. By warping the frequency axis, this branch zooms in on the 0–4000 Hz range, providing dense representations of vocal tract formants ($F_1, F_2, F_3$), vowel resonance, and natural speech prosody.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- BRANCH C -->
        <div class="branch-col col-purple">
          <span class="branch-tag tag-purple">BRANCH C · Parametric SincNet</span>
          
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">INTERNAL MATHEMATICAL MECHANICS</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li>Processes the raw waveform directly through 70 learnable 1D sinc bandpass filters:
              <div class="math-block">g(n, f_1, f_2) = 2 f_2 \text{sinc}(2\pi f_2 n) - 2 f_1 \text{sinc}(2\pi f_1 n)</div>
            </li>
            <li>Filter cutoffs $[f_{\text{low}}, f_{\text{high}}]$ are learned dynamically via backpropagation during model training.</li>
            <li>Yields a raw sub-band feature map: <code>[B, 70, 1000]</code>.</li>
            <li>Operates directly on continuous sample oscillations.</li>
          </ul>

          <div class="block-label lbl-why">WHY THIS BRANCH IS CRITICAL</div>
          <p style="font-size:12.5px; color:var(--text-body);">
            Spectrogram magnitude calculation discards raw wave phase entirely. SincNet operates directly on raw oscillating pressure waves, catching micro-timing phase jumps, frame-boundary clicking, and vocoder phase mismatches that are invisible on spectrogram images.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 3: Parallel Projection -->
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-num-badge">3</div>
        <div class="step-title-block">
          <div class="step-heading">Parallel Latent Projection &amp; 2D Spatial Attention Gating</div>
          <div class="step-tensor-sig">Dimensional Harmonization: Heterogeneous Inputs → 3 × [B, 1000, 256]</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="branch-grid">
        <div class="branch-col col-blue">
          <span class="branch-tag tag-blue">BRANCH A · Linear Projection</span>
          <p style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:12px; margin-bottom:8px;"><code>Conv2d(1, 256, kernel=(1025, 1)) → BatchNorm2d → GELU → X_lin ∈ ℝ^(B × 1000 × 256)</code></p>
          <p style="font-size:12px; color:var(--text-body);">Projects the 1025 physical frequency bins down to $d_{\text{model}} = 256$ while preserving all 1000 temporal frames. BatchNorm normalizes high-frequency energy disparity.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="branch-col col-amber">
          <span class="branch-tag tag-amber">BRANCH B · Mel Projection</span>
          <p style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:12px; margin-bottom:8px;"><code>Conv2d(1, 256, kernel=(128, 1)) → BatchNorm2d → GELU → X_mel ∈ ℝ^(B × 1000 × 256)</code></p>
          <p style="font-size:12px; color:var(--text-body);">Upsamples the 128 Mel channels to the shared 256-dimensional space. Smooth GELU activations ensure gradient stability during multi-branch backpropagation.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="branch-col col-purple">
          <span class="branch-tag tag-purple">BRANCH C · SincNet Gating</span>
          <p style="font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:12px; margin-bottom:8px;"><code>Conv1d(70, 256) ⊗ 2D Attention Mask M ∈ [0, 1] → X_sinc ∈ ℝ^(B × 1000 × 256)</code></p>
          <p style="font-size:12px; color:var(--text-body);">Applies a lightweight spatial attention gate that dampens non-vocal background pauses and room static before passing unquantized sub-band features to sequence layers.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- FLOW CONNECTOR -->
  <div class="flow-v-connector"><div class="flow-line"></div><div class="flow-arrow-head"></div></div>

  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <!-- PHASE 2: TRI-STREAM BIDIRECTIONAL MAMBA (BIMAMBA) SEQUENCE MODELING -->
  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <div class="phase-section">
    <div class="phase-banner" style="border-left-color: var(--c-amber);">
      <div class="phase-eyebrow" style="color: var(--c-amber);">PHASE 2</div>
      <div class="phase-title">Tri-Stream Bidirectional Mamba (BiMamba) Sequence Modeling</div>
      <div class="phase-goal">GOAL: Model non-causal temporal context and speech dynamics across time in linear complexity O(N), replacing quadratic Transformer self-attention without incurring the translation-invariance flaws of 2D CNNs.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- Deep Dive: Why Mamba Processes Spectrograms -->
    <div class="step-card" style="border: 1px solid rgba(242, 193, 78, 0.4);">
      <div class="block-label lbl-why">CORE ARCHITECTURAL PARADIGM: SPECTROGRAMS AS SEQUENCE EMBEDDINGS</div>
      <p style="font-size:13.5px; color:var(--text-body); margin-bottom:12px;">
        Traditional audio classifiers treat spectrograms like static JPEG images, running 2D CNN filters across them. This introduces a fatal flaw: <strong>Translation Invariance</strong>. In an image, moving a cat 50 pixels up does not change its meaning. In a spectrogram, shifting 50 pixels up transforms a $200\text{ Hz}$ vocal formant into an $8000\text{ Hz}$ hiss. 
      </p>
      <p style="font-size:13.5px; color:var(--text-body);">
        <strong>Our Approach:</strong> We project frequency into 256 dedicated channel dimensions and use <strong>Mamba as the sequential analytical brain</strong>, stepping across 1000 temporal frames in $O(N)$ linear time to evaluate how spectral channels evolve over time.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 4: Internal Mamba SSM Math -->
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-num-badge">4</div>
        <div class="step-title-block">
          <div class="step-heading">Dynamic Parameter Discretization &amp; Input-Dependent Noise Gating</div>
          <div class="step-tensor-sig">Selective State Space (SSM) Discretization inside each Mamba Block at Time Step t</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-2col">
        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-math">CONTINUOUS-TO-DISCRETE STATE SPACE EQUATIONS</div>
          <p style="font-size:12.5px; margin-bottom:6px;">A continuous State Space Model maps an input $x(t) \in \mathbb{R}^D$ to output $y(t) \in \mathbb{R}^D$ via hidden state $h(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{D \times N}$ ($D=256, N=16$):</p>
          <div class="math-block">
            h'(t) = A h(t) + B x(t)<br>
            y(t) = C h(t)
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:12.5px; margin-bottom:6px;">Mamba discretizes parameters using Zero-Order Hold (ZOH) with input-dependent step size $\Delta_t$:</p>
          <div class="math-block">
            \overline{A}_t = \exp(\Delta_t A)<br>
            \overline{B}_t = \Delta_t B_t<br>
            h_t = \overline{A}_t h_{t-1} + \overline{B}_t x_t<br>
            y_t = C_t h_t
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-failsafe">MATHEMATICAL NOISE GATING ON AUDIO FRAMES</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li><strong>Dynamic Projections:</strong> Unlike traditional RNNs or CNNs with static weights, Mamba computes parameters dynamically from each frame $x_t$:
              <div class="math-block">B_t = \text{Linear}(x_t), \quad C_t = \text{Linear}(x_t), \quad \Delta_t = \text{Softplus}(\text{Linear}(x_t))</div>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Gating Stationary Background Noise:</strong> When an incoming audio frame $x_t$ contains air-conditioner static, room hiss, or silence, the projection network drives $B_t \to 0$ and $\Delta_t \to 0$.</li>
            <li><strong>Memory Isolation:</strong> Because $\overline{B}_t \to 0$, the state update collapses to $h_t \approx h_{t-1} + 0$. <strong>Background noise is multiplied by zero and blocked from contaminating the hidden state memory $h_t$.</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 5 & 6: Parallel BiMamba Sweeps -->
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-num-badge">5-6</div>
        <div class="step-title-block">
          <div class="step-heading">Parallel Forward &amp; Backward Mamba Sweeps Across All 3 Streams</div>
          <div class="step-tensor-sig">Forward Carryover (h_0 ← h_1000^prev) &amp; Backward Zero-Init (h'_init = 0)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="branch-grid">
        
        <!-- BLUE BIMAMBA -->
        <div class="branch-col col-blue">
          <span class="branch-tag tag-blue">BRANCH A · Linear STFT BiMamba</span>
          
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">FORWARD SWEEP (t = 1 → 1000)</div>
          <p style="font-size:12px; color:var(--text-body); margin-bottom:8px;">
            Carries forward state $h_{1000}^{\text{prev}}$ from previous window. Tracks physical vocoder frame-stitching boundaries and phase continuity drift across high-frequency bins ($4000\text{--}8000\text{ Hz}$).
          </p>

          <div class="block-label lbl-how">BACKWARD SWEEP (t = 1000 → 1)</div>
          <p style="font-size:12px; color:var(--text-body); margin-bottom:8px;">
            Scans reverse sequence with $h'_{\text{init}} = 0$. Provides non-causal context on how high-frequency vocoder spikes resolve into subsequent speech frames.
          </p>

          <div class="block-label lbl-why">WHAT MEMORY STATE h_t STORES</div>
          <p style="font-size:11.5px; color:var(--text-muted);">
            Stores mathematical phase coherence, vocoder frame jitter, and narrow-band synthetic frequency anomalies.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- AMBER BIMAMBA -->
        <div class="branch-col col-amber">
          <span class="branch-tag tag-amber">BRANCH B · Log-Mel BiMamba</span>
          
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">FORWARD SWEEP (t = 1 → 1000)</div>
          <p style="font-size:12px; color:var(--text-body); margin-bottom:8px;">
            Carries forward state $h_{1000}^{\text{prev}}$. Models long-range vocal tract formant glides ($F_1, F_2, F_3$), prosodic speaking cadence, and fundamental frequency ($F_0$) drift over minutes of conversation.
          </p>

          <div class="block-label lbl-how">BACKWARD SWEEP (t = 1000 → 1)</div>
          <p style="font-size:12px; color:var(--text-body); margin-bottom:8px;">
            Scans reverse sequence with $h'_{\text{init}} = 0$. Analyzes whether syllable durations and 40ms pauses resolve naturally into upcoming phonemes within the 10s horizon.
          </p>

          <div class="block-label lbl-why">WHAT MEMORY STATE h_t STORES</div>
          <p style="font-size:11.5px; color:var(--text-muted);">
            Stores biological vocal resonance, phoneme duration trajectories, and intonation stress patterns.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- PURPLE BIMAMBA -->
        <div class="branch-col col-purple">
          <span class="branch-tag tag-purple">BRANCH C · SincNet BiMamba</span>
          
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">FORWARD SWEEP (t = 1 → 1000)</div>
          <p style="font-size:12px; color:var(--text-body); margin-bottom:8px;">
            Carries forward state $h_{1000}^{\text{prev}}$. Models continuous unquantized waveform envelope oscillations and sub-band transient dynamics directly in the time domain.
          </p>

          <div class="block-label lbl-how">BACKWARD SWEEP (t = 1000 → 1)</div>
          <p style="font-size:12px; color:var(--text-body); margin-bottom:8px;">
            Scans reverse sequence with $h'_{\text{init}} = 0$. Verifies whether raw sample-level waveform phase spikes align with upcoming speech energy transitions.
          </p>

          <div class="block-label lbl-why">WHAT MEMORY STATE h_t STORES</div>
          <p style="font-size:11.5px; color:var(--text-muted);">
            Stores sample-level wave phase continuity, boundary clicking, and transient sub-band oscillations.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <!-- Step 7 -->
      <div class="deep-dive-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-title">Step 7: Sweep Concatenation &amp; Residual Fusion (Per Branch)</div>
        <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--text-body); margin-bottom:6px;">
          For each independent branch, forward and reversed sweeps are concatenated into 512 dimensions, projected back to 256 dimensions, and added to the original input via a residual skip connection:
        </p>
        <div class="math-block">
          H_{\text{branch}} = \text{Linear}\Big(\big[Y_{\text{forward}} \parallel \text{Flip}(Y_{\text{backward}})\big]\Big) + X_{\text{branch}} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times 1000 \times 256}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

